#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128855 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129277 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3337f1e5-3b55-5e56-8003-d80e2a1293c2" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/id #uuid "64c86335-cc36-5b99-a8e3-f2658435b29b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12401" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5a19eefe8fdcd63ef9f8a35bf4517b022ab2c35b5fccd06e1e797f67e567653a.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R  f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\int_0^1\\int_{\\sqrt{z}}^1\\int_0^{y^2-z} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y.$" :question/worked-solution "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: \\sqrt{z} \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq y^2-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\int_a^b\\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)}\\int_{u_1(y,z)}^{u_2(y,z)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\int_a^b\\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)}\\int_0^{y^2-z} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/ec/ec195fe4aa1bec5221ec25a1921dfe36e78db34ad3e295fec541c1d05a85d60d.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: \\sqrt{z} \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z$ to $\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{z}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nz = y^2\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z\\leq y^2 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/84/841f014cae7bb224b6f743a86583cb0a70a423d60176b2efd6482d155aa020bc.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\n\\int_0^1 \\int_{0}^{y^2} \\int_0^{y^2-z} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128857 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration When the Projection Is Partitioned"}] :question/id #uuid "f8b32b74-b82a-5c07-b244-945189e2bcc3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12403" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $z,$ then $y,$ and then $x,$ express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\int_{-1}^0 \\int_{y^2}^{1} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y\n+\n\\int_0^1 \\int_{y}^{1} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)}  f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integrals, we see that the domain of integration $R = R_1\\cup R_2,$ where $R_1$ and $R_2$ are type I regions, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 0,\\,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x-1\\le z\\le {y}^{2}\\}, \\\\ {R}_{2} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,y\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x-1\\le z\\le {y}^{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/c8/c8bfd933472e1944f63b1295bda7fc324311ea2ca2ada3636e9f5ab82e4e5fc5.png)\n\nThe region $D$ consists of two type II regions in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD =\n\\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 0, \\quad y^2 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n\\:\\cup\\:\n\\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad y \\leq x \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the lower boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=y^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny = -\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nand the upper boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=y\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny =x.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad -\\sqrt{x} \\leq y \\leq  x \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c7/c73a0e30d6996490f9f41bf3e8777c14d542755387f643ad464bd1dd6cab2839.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1}} \\int_{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{-\\sqrt{x}}}^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{x}} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128858 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Triple Integrals by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/id #uuid "72795f5f-0d15-52e1-bfd8-997ae8e20265" :question/math-academy-id "e-12404" :question/problem "Evaluate\n$\\displaystyle\n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{y}^{1} \\int_{0}^{y} 3e^{x^3} \\: \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: Change change the order of integration to $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} 3e^{x^3} \\:\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: y \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq y \\right\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{0}^{y} 3e^{x^3} \\:\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdd856ef8a7940a009b7ee27d6614fdb8c042e6655c2316ce4db0633bfd04e9f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad y \\leq x \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $y = y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx = y \\qquad\\Longrightarrow \\qquad y = x\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\ny = 0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq x \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/1e/1e28e1cef9743da80d6fd2d38840810b12239d8546254c4d4cca767568f76a53.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{y}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}[z]_{0}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3y{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[{e}^{{x}^{3}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration When the Projection Is Partitioned"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "270fc9a3-6745-5ea1-a800-6128a441ff7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\,\\,\\,q=y,\\,\\,\\,r=\\sqrt{-y-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553942} {:answer/id #uuid "4779f108-0a30-5e16-95a9-540802d12ecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=0,\\,\\,\\,q=\\sqrt{-y-1},\\,\\,\\,r=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554005} {:answer/id #uuid "53105811-6f7d-5823-80d4-62ba7e36f98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=0,\\,\\,\\,q=y,\\,\\,\\,r=\\sqrt{-y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554030} {:answer/id #uuid "931c1007-7643-5d0e-86ab-07276a0fd174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\,\\,\\,q=\\sqrt{-y-1},\\,\\,\\,r=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554195} {:answer/id #uuid "aab8905a-7a25-5c53-8b0c-6d625197c8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\,\\,\\,q=\\sqrt{-y+1},\\,\\,\\,r=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "931c1007-7643-5d0e-86ab-07276a0fd174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\,\\,\\,q=\\sqrt{-y-1},\\,\\,\\,r=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554195} :answer-field/id #uuid "5209110b-3a7c-51ef-9fce-1dfc5ef674ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78810200-6809-5a82-a224-a2c79dd59842" :question/math-academy-id "q-161524" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-{z}^{2}-1}^{-z}{\\int }_{0}^{2y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{-2}^{p}{\\int }_{q}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{r}{\\int }_{0}^{2y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(y)}^{{v}_{4}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(y,z)}^{{u}_{4}(y,z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,-{z}^{2}-1\\le y\\le -z,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2y+z\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-{z}^{2}-1}^{-z}{\\int }_{0}^{2y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{2y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(y)}^{{v}_{4}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{2y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/59/59e5dae7cb1d621213f3e4ef792623bdfd9a42aef19c75f7ca9d65b3af32eeda.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,-{z}^{2}-1\\le y\\le -z\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we split $D$ into two type I regions.\n\n![](images/9c/9c968af98710367752a9d788b9b4c00d8c04e31d5494f7887ea5d2d635312338.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the left region, written in the form $z=z(y),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $z=\\sqrt{-y-1}\\,$ and $\\,z=1.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the right region, written in the form $z=z(y),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $z=0\\,$ and $\\,z=-y.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le -1,\\,\\sqrt{-y-1}\\le z\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 0,\\,0\\le z\\le -y\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{-y-1}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{-y}{\\int }_{0}^{2y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129277 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3337f1e5-3b55-5e56-8003-d80e2a1293c2" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2d703e6-4743-5763-af25-20bf0c6a5dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389265} {:answer/id #uuid "6899558c-7e4c-5da5-a914-cf18f2f7181e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389266} {:answer/id #uuid "30f7302b-d1a7-5760-a118-699f680c9108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+y}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389267} {:answer/id #uuid "c2677da7-49ce-541f-9b50-f661e63780a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+y}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389268} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3e861e-41d1-5867-9af6-c49dba5bb8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a3e861e-41d1-5867-9af6-c49dba5bb8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389269} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5e881a1-cf29-5a9e-9fbe-33a59f952017" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "318d728a-5f9a-5d33-846c-76ae520cec78" :question/math-academy-id "q-162226" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c420d45ca9418fd29889d91a49cce4ae80bb71bdfd4e4aa701b495cc8d87335f.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $z,$ then $x,$ and then $y.$" :question/worked-solution "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+y\\}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/8b/8b7cfa72ed3dbfb121039c6e64b843d83489878a9f457fb1386e455e7d9649ec.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f49af5da2e4f1f281700e16c4659fabfcd0b15661947ca985d38f208f8563877.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration When the Projection Is Partitioned"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "463d28ca-1df1-50c0-85de-534a393326de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2y}^{2y+3}{\\int }_{-y}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554001} {:answer/id #uuid "668be39c-6dba-5a63-9f61-5cb30fd0c100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y/2}^{3+y}{\\int }_{-y}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554071} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2ad9b6-2325-5d04-8b7b-dc7feb79acf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{2y}^{3-y}{\\int }_{-y}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554086} {:answer/id #uuid "a908daaf-d2d3-5c7e-a2b3-0e34b598e588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{3-y}{\\int }_{-y}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554244} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8e0a76-12f6-5235-8928-a55923107f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/2}^{3-2y}{\\int }_{-y}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a908daaf-d2d3-5c7e-a2b3-0e34b598e588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{3-y}{\\int }_{-y}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554244} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9447dc2-1b9f-505d-ae0d-6cd7412e4910" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7da71e9d-63e8-5f19-957f-ca4c328a0eb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-162234" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y,$ express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2z}{\\int }_{-y}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-z}{\\int }_{-y}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integrals, we see that the domain of integration $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2},$ where ${R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{2}$ are type II regions, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2z,\\,\\,-y\\le x\\le z\\}, \\\\ {R}_{2} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-z,\\,\\,-y\\le x\\le z\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{-y}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/9b/9b8806738b61c4ce692f555261abcd9918096a7585291954a7f11c39aadeab39.png)\n\nThe region $D$ consists of two type II regions in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2z\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-z\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=2z\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{y}{2}\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=3-z\\,⟹\\,z=3-y.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le z\\le 3-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d1/d1a9d83672ffbd372b6a994c62771291d957e6a5c13189a6baea6d544f6a1bbc.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{3-y}{\\int }_{-y}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration When the Projection Is Partitioned"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b1c78bb-abf4-5100-b1f9-019fa662400c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2{y}^{2}}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278841} {:answer/id #uuid "1cba1981-12b1-5414-b9d8-fe923afe1062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2y}}^{{y}^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278857} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6c0c6e-1233-590e-8516-26b9696bb8c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{y/2}}^{{y}^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279577} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca5f9d3-3631-55a8-a3c9-6f813114bee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2y}}^{{y}^{2}}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279986} {:answer/id #uuid "f71f69d6-539e-5fa4-b0c6-31b41984e42e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{y/2}}^{2y}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c6c0c6e-1233-590e-8516-26b9696bb8c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{y/2}}^{{y}^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279577} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7f04013-8ec1-5ee6-b6f6-ce50bcb12012" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14061a2e-2a76-5b88-b932-249bcad9073d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162247" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $z,$ then $x,$ and then $y,$ express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-2{x}^{3}}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2x}}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integrals, we see that the domain of integration $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2},$ where ${R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{2}$ are type I regions, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,-2{x}^{3}\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,-y\\le z\\le x+3\\}, \\\\ {R}_{2} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,\\sqrt{2x}\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,-y\\le z\\le x+3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/83/834238127fb9a55c956b2dd708e1dc7d925c63725990003eff6aac28ee42a873.png)\n\nThe region $D$ consists of two type I regions in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-2{x}^{3}\\le y\\le 2\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{2x}\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=-2{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=-\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{2x}\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/82/82bddc0c83e0e5a5824b7525bcd4fd2dc9b8f88473dfe1fd5c6cdb2ff0cefcaf.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{y/2}}^{{y}^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration When the Projection Is Partitioned"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e1b4b28-86f4-57cf-ae63-c12f58077683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{6-{z}^{2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279453} {:answer/id #uuid "6233b703-4e20-5217-96bb-e8deb7ab6679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{z-2}^{6-z}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279484} {:answer/id #uuid "73eeb410-78d4-58a8-9a45-e433cfc9bd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}+1}^{6-\\sqrt{z}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279639} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b7cb68-94c3-56c8-b0ff-66aeef3e6ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2{z}^{2}}^{6+{z}^{2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280499} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbff4b5-819d-5e69-9452-eda2ce5bd5e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-2}^{6-{z}^{2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfbff4b5-819d-5e69-9452-eda2ce5bd5e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-2}^{6-{z}^{2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280565} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a0d8a65-88f8-596a-8347-a4b79a95d6e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c5dd846-0a3a-5a82-a678-28ac12606de8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162249" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $x,$ then $y,$ and then $z,$ express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y+2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6-y}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integrals, we see that the domain of integration $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2},$ where ${R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{2}$ are type I regions, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{y+2},\\,\\,-z\\le x\\le {y}^{2}\\}, \\\\ {R}_{2} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,2\\le y\\le 6,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{6-y},\\,\\,-z\\le x\\le {y}^{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/aa/aa1c884900c3cb01a719dc6d22604469984d2b08291d9d925b8f37d794d455b2.png)\n\nThe region $D$ consists of two type I regions in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{y+2}\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 6,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{6-y}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{y+2}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{6-y}\\,⟹\\,y=6-{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,{z}^{2}-2\\le y\\le 6-{z}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4139dde4d56e83b91e4a72d42ccdf296ad20773520ccdaa51d7a84b9e1a3f31.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-2}^{6-{z}^{2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Triple Integrals by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e74a82f-4bd7-5c2f-8751-ac19f153eecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278722} {:answer/id #uuid "90db29e8-ff5c-5044-9543-fee4992d484f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279884} {:answer/id #uuid "98b9f9a0-15c1-5dd0-a2e6-9d749f3ab244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279942} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b2e64a-c3e7-5882-8acf-3672f33bf2ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280082} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd49d7b-3fa3-52dd-9351-bc6320abedca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfd49d7b-3fa3-52dd-9351-bc6320abedca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280277} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c737135-2cdd-59c3-a038-3b9c326dc776" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90d38ce0-881b-51da-a009-3b19a6113351" :question/math-academy-id "q-162250" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le {e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/38/38eb7316b1ee5c947eee37a9cc10530ff08f793b7564cf0d88c6ce24e1239f9d.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/24/241abff7fae5d6ba3301d605ff4c4e62ff61e423515c7bc3a54b17b9aec525aa.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}[{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}[z]_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}[y]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi /2}-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}) \\\\  & =-(1-e) \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Triple Integrals by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "562d0704-f8bb-56d1-ad51-2efebd134efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279380} {:answer/id #uuid "65d6262f-31f6-5c6a-a73b-e3a7b55728a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279520} {:answer/id #uuid "d301c278-fecb-50eb-a7b0-cf15bf32c993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280453} {:answer/id #uuid "d5144876-7c4f-5ef7-b912-312e36e6a5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280471} {:answer/id #uuid "f7eac3ef-b262-53cc-9f03-528d3cd26d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5144876-7c4f-5ef7-b912-312e36e6a5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280471} :answer-field/id #uuid "35b87413-958f-55b3-b6a9-0b06c650f7b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c00cd44a-d34c-5f6e-aacf-b4ef475837d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162253" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{z}\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {e}^{z/x}\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/e8/e83c249a6abf9710a201462e16c646a11411dbcdbca46f34e9beb8d2db7a654b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\sqrt{z}\\le x\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $z=z(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{z}\\,⟹\\,z={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le {x}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7e/7e684766b85a214d1f1dd7845979052c532e11ecce74d0284b8fc379b5669957.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}[y]_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{e}^{z/x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{e}^{z/x}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x{e}^{z/x}]_{z=0}^{z={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{e}^{x}-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[(x-1){e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(0-\\frac{1}{2})-(-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Triple Integrals by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "448ae3db-641d-5f64-a5c9-14f4cedec775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553995} {:answer/id #uuid "482bfb85-d1a0-5e37-9c53-93569bba7aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554007} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d6645f-466a-52fe-b330-b115e7efc0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554264} {:answer/id #uuid "c5440dc0-7eb1-5868-85fb-0761762da9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554300} {:answer/id #uuid "df1540ff-dda7-54f2-9afe-827410ca5f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5440dc0-7eb1-5868-85fb-0761762da9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554300} :answer-field/id #uuid "46815972-0754-5def-987e-4a8b3dd30f2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f35d7c5d-0e2d-5ee0-b1c5-16c26213b1d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162326" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed7308cf6c77f5d89b94ec90237fa9161fc85429182813f38b55d916bdbdaeef.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $y=y(z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡z.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/4f/4fb3323b2c295df8351910890f20266840b131a4dfd8a7bba0d61a3eb03bea23.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}[\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,[x]_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,[y]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{3}{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡z)}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[(1+0)^{3/2}-(1+1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(1-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129277 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3337f1e5-3b55-5e56-8003-d80e2a1293c2" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a416a83-b992-5dea-933e-d7994ee0e1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-z}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553888} {:answer/id #uuid "57530fa0-a8c6-5fa8-b434-155da990f33e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554040} {:answer/id #uuid "619538c5-7b67-52bb-8a52-0a0ef0d09a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-z}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2-y-z}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554060} {:answer/id #uuid "739cb01f-393c-57bf-a010-d144513405ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554103} {:answer/id #uuid "93a3db56-671e-5488-9051-35f5266d1834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2-y-z}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57530fa0-a8c6-5fa8-b434-155da990f33e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554040} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f2f208-bfdf-5d7a-b0b7-7f94fe8a16ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31b80dba-d43f-591d-b1ec-e6cb3d1597c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-162327" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4573a2e3c7b220ac363c996c5332a3db93cc48b5b941e46a529b6912804f0c6.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{y}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $y,$ and then $z.$" :question/worked-solution "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-{y}^{2},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2-y-z\\}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/8d/8d1db6e886f00596814900ca0c4113590a37a407b65c800eb1f9926328ade50c.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-{y}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ is \n\n$$\nz=1-{y}^{2}\\,⟹\\,y=\\sqrt{1-z}.\n$$\n\nand the left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-z}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1fe8a810a7e98c8a40739d5019ff9054a5375c0a12ae7fd1b17f8b6c8bb19f75.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554646 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129277 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3337f1e5-3b55-5e56-8003-d80e2a1293c2" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4955968f-08d9-5d5e-90f4-c1643ad1847e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1/y}{\\int }_{0}^{4/z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554010} {:answer/id #uuid "7aef16c7-4821-502c-a25f-730651588c0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4/y}{\\int }_{0}^{4-yz}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554117} {:answer/id #uuid "ac21b4ed-6e73-52e4-9f6c-294e04f1814b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-yz}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554252} {:answer/id #uuid "cacc640b-c32f-5efa-abb2-b66482f7bb2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4/y}{\\int }_{0}^{4/z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554315} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb0941d-3cc4-5879-87b3-21a7dac6fa5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1/y}{\\int }_{0}^{4-yz}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aef16c7-4821-502c-a25f-730651588c0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4/y}{\\int }_{0}^{4-yz}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554117} :answer-field/id #uuid "d084d670-2e7b-5600-a90e-83f0ea45658b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fff4e417-ea88-59e6-bd63-239378f4d2c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-162329" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fc551f1439ed5a551ae8fd18234479bb062d43ae9e4faf55d896739f76e0f4d.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4/z}{\\int }_{0}^{4-yz}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y.$" :question/worked-solution "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 4,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{z},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-yz\\}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{4-yz}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/9a/9a59198b60398e2b3044296c3cc76b4748785303029dccc1087320cd11cb7d55.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 4,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{z}\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{4}{z}\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{4}{y}\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\,1\\le z\\le \\frac{4}{y}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/9b/9bc88aa4f0ad07d89ff5dd3c0de16208cb8bf47d774b557a7bf2140790c95291.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4/y}{\\int }_{0}^{4-yz}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Triple Integrals by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0073c988-ab13-5d35-82dc-f86a2f72a0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278588} {:answer/id #uuid "268b4c33-c226-5773-b9b1-46201fc827d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278936} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc11660-2038-5011-af7b-4ccf1db2e5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi -1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279836} {:answer/id #uuid "c41bff5d-e1eb-5b17-a3c4-b0bc834579fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280318} {:answer/id #uuid "c71ff19f-ae04-593f-8810-e601bcf14f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0073c988-ab13-5d35-82dc-f86a2f72a0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278588} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd81652e-0bcb-5dc4-b49d-1d1e352b840b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55c78036-de48-5eea-9253-f540a61a0813" :question/math-academy-id "q-162330" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{arccos}⁡y,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c5f4ec9b9b12411f3057bc074a14a1f541c768c30d3a9746d7a0484b0491ad2e.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{arccos}⁡y\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $y=y(z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/07/077fc8133673cd57aba570cca9a6e0f5f25aa742720948c46db2b9e1d05add69.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}y\\,[x]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}2y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{y}^{2}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2z)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[z+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2z]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c9eee90-f971-51b3-ba5c-dec986388b62" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration When the Projection Is Partitioned"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f703bd0-2dd8-50a8-84be-59480e209b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r={z}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401331} {:answer/id #uuid "16f6fbe8-a535-5960-a6c7-b5dd576f6b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r=4-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401332} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9b1939-7256-56eb-8c7a-9b505da6122c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\,\\,q=1,\\,\\,\\,r={z}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401333} {:answer/id #uuid "e24c4541-f269-56ad-b95a-224e52a4e088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\,\\,q=3,\\,\\,\\,r=4-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401334} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a313c4-3307-5cf2-9cf8-be47e0fcc064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=1,\\,\\,\\,r=z-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16f6fbe8-a535-5960-a6c7-b5dd576f6b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r=4-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401332} :answer-field/id #uuid "f079fa33-36ea-54da-8c9a-cfed3ba497cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f9c68a00-265c-5c7e-8372-6f380b398f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162332" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2y}-1}^{2-y/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-1}^{p}{\\int }_{0}^{(z+1)^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{q}{\\int }_{0}^{r}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(xy,z)}^{{u}_{4}(y,z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,\\sqrt{2y}-1\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{y}{2},\\,\\,-2z\\le x\\le y+3\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2y}-1}^{2-y/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(x)}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4de9d9df51efcec67253a1c7118466f29459ffd5c9136c144d33500ec5557bc.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{2y}-1\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{y}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we split $D$ into two type II regions.\n\n![](images/18/18bb3e55f076e022176bb60df27d5665ed2d91d75d618969c04ce192378db922.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the lower region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(z+1)^{2}.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the upper region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=4-2z.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}(z+1)^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-2z\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{(z+1)^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-2z}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbc25e04-f750-5d25-829a-ea061f010502" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Triple Integrals by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48e2a033-443c-584c-a15f-eea6d9dae46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279242} {:answer/id #uuid "642f63b8-b430-5120-8afa-60337721d5a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279503} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d73ed0-76b2-520e-9de6-dc0f038550ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280698} {:answer/id #uuid "f5cf9286-3e28-5594-96ef-d23587fa7550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (e-2)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280744} {:answer/id #uuid "f899ec44-2bfd-5d46-ac07-d2e8f6a0ae6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5cf9286-3e28-5594-96ef-d23587fa7550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (e-2)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280744} :answer-field/id #uuid "06018cfb-ebc1-54fb-ba81-4655903daaf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11b88039-dcd3-5fe3-9ad3-e8f58ab164bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-162334" :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$*" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le e,\\,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{3}y\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/14/14c67512e143995cbfcfcf9e80c8d0975bbbc42368c97aa29956bd2d3f3454a8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le e,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nz=1\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $z=z(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\,⟹\\,z={e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le {e}^{y}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d1/d18470b45d21b4734187c1f639784a4e75924ed212e3fa4c7a214d19eaeb6022.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}{[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{3}y}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\pi }{3}[z]_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\pi }{3}({e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}[{e}^{y}-y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(e-1-1+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (e-2)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129277 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3337f1e5-3b55-5e56-8003-d80e2a1293c2" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043bca1a-2206-51e9-a120-247e71cc51ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2x/3}{\\int }_{8-4x/3-2z}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553876} {:answer/id #uuid "4a550c5a-2b5d-5ffe-9202-bcc3022eb22c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{3-x/2}^{1}{\\int }_{8-4x/3-2z}^{8}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554013} {:answer/id #uuid "50146f2b-26fb-5cad-a0d7-79d0f7cde1c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2-2x/3}{\\int }_{8-4x/3-2z}^{8}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554023} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe2811f-8eb8-50fc-a883-7924a3888848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{2-2x/3}^{2}{\\int }_{8-4x/3-2z}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554383} {:answer/id #uuid "fba6c7d9-e4bf-58a1-9d10-b0e3db0b7fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3-x/2}{\\int }_{8-4x/3-2z}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebe2811f-8eb8-50fc-a883-7924a3888848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{2-2x/3}^{2}{\\int }_{8-4x/3-2z}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554383} :answer-field/id #uuid "513d3730-41ea-5d79-9d65-fe4f1ea03b21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac81bb63-6896-5260-96a8-5a88cb11f40d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162495" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f95783c1e9624e58b4cd26764e94fb61d45c7aa75260ce8ca726a6c2e5db9ab.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{3-3z/2}^{3}{\\int }_{8-4x/3-2z}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ and then $x.$" :question/worked-solution "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,3-\\frac{3z}{2}\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,8-\\frac{4x}{3}-2z\\le y\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{8-4x/3-2z}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/85/85d184f328ebdbed5faf2d1c43261940a8241b6637450ab5fe1c8d803026e273.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,3-\\frac{3z}{2}\\le x\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom  boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x),$ is \n\n$$\nx=3-\\frac{3z}{2}\\,⟹\\,z=2-\\frac{2x}{3}\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=2.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,2-\\frac{2x}{3}\\le z\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/6a/6ac58e4c98c79a1a698d052479b55a45dd2b79bed4ca11422d172fe306dac15f.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{2-2x/3}^{2}{\\int }_{8-4/3x-2z}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129277 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3337f1e5-3b55-5e56-8003-d80e2a1293c2" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration by Changing the Projection Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37252d37-fbb7-5a9b-9205-8a39f1053ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{1+y-z}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279073} {:answer/id #uuid "56f82c06-1cf3-56f3-9132-350791a81098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{1+y-z}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279389} {:answer/id #uuid "77c1d8e3-cee5-53e8-a3f2-413cd9ad5858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{1+y}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279661} {:answer/id #uuid "a799605e-5af1-576b-9293-31d120fa603e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280076} {:answer/id #uuid "b775de0b-50d3-5357-b6b2-3d151fdc8555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b775de0b-50d3-5357-b6b2-3d151fdc8555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280204} :answer-field/id #uuid "f24cfac2-bae7-5843-8bff-4b773654c28e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b08713a3-4d0e-55df-a71c-6cc156f3ac58" :question/math-academy-id "q-162527" :question/problem "![](images/02/024df0fe0a965feba2177adfac8bbd658fc90f4775a4707f13eba1ef2cb94702.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y.$" :question/worked-solution "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{z},\\,\\,1+y-z\\le x\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8fff232443e6da697862c2e85a9d3abfe0554b80c2fcbfa8c93de42b94a45691.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{z}\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{z}\\,⟹\\,z={y}^{3}\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,{y}^{3}\\le z\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/72/726ff420cae25c40f212714141a134fab84633cca7f49bdd268f0668d5248766.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"}]}
